WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:03.278
You have considered the person's experience and the path

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.678
for communication assistance. Now take a closer look at

3
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:11.011
the information. Standard eight addresses understandable digital and print

4
00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:14.256
materials and signage in languages used by people served.

5
00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:17.522
The principal standard keeps the person's communication needs at

6
00:00:17.544 --> 00:00:17.944
the center.

7
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:21.778
We will use five questions: can the person find

8
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.467
it, access it, understand it, use it and act

9
00:00:25.489 --> 00:00:28.311
on it? These questions are a practical review aid.

10
00:00:28.578 --> 00:00:32.422
They do not replace technical accessibility testing, translation review

11
00:00:32.489 --> 00:00:35.911
or applicable agency requirements. They help you recognize where

12
00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:36.978
further work is needed.

13
00:00:37.544 --> 00:00:40.789
Choose a public-facing or non-sensitive example for this activity.

14
00:00:40.811 --> 00:00:44.767
If you are reviewing a real work document, keep

15
00:00:44.789 --> 00:00:47.567
it in the appropriate work location rather than placing

16
00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:49.922
personal information in your learning notes.

17
00:00:50.952 --> 00:00:54.686
First, can the person find the information? A statement

18
00:00:54.708 --> 00:00:57.974
about assistance may exist somewhere on a website but

19
00:00:57.997 --> 00:01:00.486
be absent from the notice where the person needs

20
00:01:00.508 --> 00:01:03.930
it. A link may use an internal document name

21
00:01:03.952 --> 00:01:07.441
that gives no clue about its purpose. A telephone

22
00:01:07.463 --> 00:01:10.819
option may be difficult to locate among several unrelated

23
00:01:10.841 --> 00:01:11.486
numbers.

24
00:01:12.258 --> 00:01:16.147
Next, can the person access it? Consider format and

25
00:01:16.169 --> 00:01:18.702
method, not only whether the file opens on your

26
00:01:18.724 --> 00:01:21.769
computer. An image of text may be unreadable to

27
00:01:21.791 --> 00:01:25.924
assistive technology. A video without accurate captions or a

28
00:01:25.947 --> 00:01:29.524
useful transcript may exclude people. A form that requires

29
00:01:29.547 --> 00:01:33.013
a mouse can block keyboard users. A digital-only instruction

30
00:01:33.036 --> 00:01:36.013
may be unusable without reliable access to a suitable

31
00:01:36.036 --> 00:01:37.147
device or connection.

32
00:01:37.824 --> 00:01:41.002
Do not assume you can determine every accessibility issue

33
00:01:41.024 --> 00:01:43.913
by looking at the screen. Use the established review

34
00:01:43.936 --> 00:01:47.847
process and involve the appropriate accessibility support. If you

35
00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:52.069
create documents, use meaningful headings, descriptive links, readable structure

36
00:01:52.402 --> 00:01:55.069
and the approved tools for checking the result. Check

37
00:01:55.091 --> 00:01:57.980
the document that people will actually receive, including any

38
00:01:58.002 --> 00:01:58.913
exported version.

39
00:02:00.102 --> 00:02:03.791
Look at our sample notice. It asks the person

40
00:02:03.813 --> 00:02:07.169
to attend a service-planning meeting, but the opening sentence

41
00:02:07.191 --> 00:02:12.413
is filled with acronyms and internal references. The reader

42
00:02:12.436 --> 00:02:15.080
may not know what the meeting is for, whether

43
00:02:15.102 --> 00:02:17.191
they have a choice, or what they need to

44
00:02:17.213 --> 00:02:17.747
do next.

45
00:02:18.351 --> 00:02:22.596
A clearer structure begins with purpose and action. Explain

46
00:02:22.618 --> 00:02:25.462
why the person is receiving the notice. Identify the

47
00:02:25.484 --> 00:02:27.862
next step, the relevant date and a way to

48
00:02:27.884 --> 00:02:31.796
ask questions or request assistance. Explain necessary terms when

49
00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:35.262
they first appear. Keep essential rights, choices and requirements

50
00:02:35.284 --> 00:02:38.751
intact; plain language is not permission to remove important

51
00:02:38.773 --> 00:02:39.151
meaning.

52
00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:43.562
Translation and plain language solve related but different problems.

53
00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:47.140
A translated document can still be difficult to understand

54
00:02:47.162 --> 00:02:50.807
if the source is unnecessarily complicated. An easy-to-read English

55
00:02:50.829 --> 00:02:53.762
version does not meet every language need. Use the

56
00:02:53.784 --> 00:02:57.340
appropriate qualified review and approved process for the actual

57
00:02:57.362 --> 00:03:01.473
audience and purpose. Do not send confidential information into

58
00:03:01.496 --> 00:03:03.296
an unapproved translation service.

59
00:03:04.329 --> 00:03:07.440
Now ask whether the person can use the information

60
00:03:07.462 --> 00:03:11.062
to make a decision or complete a step. A

61
00:03:11.084 --> 00:03:14.018
notice might say to contact the department without naming

62
00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.307
a usable route. A form might explain what is

63
00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:20.396
needed but provide no way to ask for help.

64
00:03:21.218 --> 00:03:24.484
A deadline might be stated without explaining what action

65
00:03:24.507 --> 00:03:26.729
must be completed by that date.

66
00:03:27.294 --> 00:03:30.672
Make the next step concrete. In our learning example,

67
00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:34.072
a useful notice would explain the meeting's purpose, how

68
00:03:34.094 --> 00:03:37.028
to confirm or discuss the appointment, how to request

69
00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:41.161
communication assistance, and where to ask questions. Use actual

70
00:03:41.183 --> 00:03:44.517
approved contact details in real work. We are not

71
00:03:44.539 --> 00:03:46.739
inventing a new contact or process here.

72
00:03:47.322 --> 00:03:50.544
Check that the proposed change is within your authority.

73
00:03:51.122 --> 00:03:53.122
You may be able to improve a draft, but

74
00:03:53.144 --> 00:03:56.700
a regulated notice or official form may require review

75
00:03:56.722 --> 00:04:00.567
before use. Preserve the required content and route the

76
00:04:00.589 --> 00:04:03.078
suggested revision to the person responsible.

77
00:04:04.346 --> 00:04:07.901
In the activity, compare three proposed revisions to the

78
00:04:07.923 --> 00:04:10.701
notice. Think about what each option would mean for

79
00:04:10.723 --> 00:04:13.390
someone who needs to understand the information and act

80
00:04:13.412 --> 00:04:17.746
on it. Consider clarity, accessibility, required meaning and a

81
00:04:17.768 --> 00:04:21.234
way to request assistance. Choose the strongest revision, then

82
00:04:21.257 --> 00:04:21.946
read the feedback.

83
00:04:22.712 --> 00:04:26.446
Then apply the five questions to your example. You

84
00:04:26.468 --> 00:04:30.023
do not have to solve every issue today. Identify

85
00:04:30.046 --> 00:04:33.112
one change you can propose or make, one item

86
00:04:33.134 --> 00:04:36.157
that needs specialist review, and one way to check

87
00:04:36.179 --> 00:04:38.757
whether the revised information is more usable.

88
00:04:39.484 --> 00:04:42.818
Where appropriate, feedback from people who use the information

89
00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.796
can reveal issues that an internal review misses. That

90
00:04:46.818 --> 00:04:50.551
feedback should be accessible and voluntary, with a clear

91
00:04:50.573 --> 00:04:53.218
explanation of how it will be used. We will

92
00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.773
develop that approach in the community-partnership lesson.

93
00:04:56.727 --> 00:04:59.682
Save a short description of the improvement in your

94
00:04:59.704 --> 00:05:03.327
own notes: the barrier, the proposed change, the necessary

95
00:05:03.349 --> 00:05:06.904
review and the expected benefit. Avoid claiming that a

96
00:05:06.927 --> 00:05:09.993
document is fully accessible simply because it passed one

97
00:05:10.016 --> 00:05:12.816
automated check or received a positive comment.

98
00:05:13.503 --> 00:05:17.214
The goal is a more dependable experience. In the

99
00:05:17.237 --> 00:05:20.303
next lesson, we will connect your proposed improvement to

100
00:05:20.326 --> 00:05:23.570
the roles, resources and decisions that make it possible

101
00:05:23.592 --> 00:05:24.459
to sustain.
